<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<task id="019" project="spaarkeai-word-add-in-r1">
  <metadata>
    <title>FR-02 pre-flight: confirm the custom-XML markup-vs-parts premise and close the WordApi 1.4 manifest gap</title>
    <phase>0 De-risk and baseline</phase>
    <gate>startable</gate>
    <status>completed</status>
    <rigor>FULL</rigor>
    <rigor-reason>Modifies both Word manifests (install-time contract) and settles a premise that FR-02's entire design rests on. A wrong answer here means task 014 builds an identity mechanism that cannot run.</rigor-reason>
    <model-tier>opus</model-tier>
    <model-tier-reason>Reconciling Office.js API-set semantics against manifest declarations, where the failure mode is a clean install followed by a runtime failure. This exact class has already bitten this project twice.</model-tier-reason>
    <effort>high</effort>
    <parallel-group>none</parallel-group>
    <parallel-safe>false</parallel-safe>
    <parallel-reason>Edits `word/manifest.json` and `word/word-manifest.xml`, which task 011 also owns and which is still open pending deploy verification.</parallel-reason>
    <deps>002</deps>
    <tags>office-addins, word, manifest, document-identity, spike, phase-0</tags>
    <estimated-effort>3-5 hours</estimated-effort>
  </metadata>

  <prompt>Close the two FR-02 blockers Spike-1 surfaced: the unconfirmed custom-XML markup-vs-parts premise, and the manifest declaring WordApi 1.3 while `customXmlParts` requires 1.4. Both must be settled BEFORE task 014 builds anything.</prompt>

  <role>SPAARKE platform developer. Office.js requirement sets, the unified manifest, and Word custom XML parts. Follow ADRs strictly.</role>

  <goal>The Word manifests declare what the stamp mechanism actually needs, the markup-vs-parts premise is confirmed or refuted with evidence, and task 014 either has a green light or a documented reason it cannot proceed as designed.</goal>

  <context>
    <background>
      Both items were found by Spike-1 (task 002, 2026-09-09) — see `notes/spikes/spike-1-document-url.md`.

      **(1) The manifest gap — verified, live, fixable with no host.** `Word.Document.customXmlParts` AND `Word.Document.settings` are both `[Api set: WordApi 1.4]` in `@types/office-js`, while BOTH `word/manifest.json` and `word/word-manifest.xml` declare WordApi **1.3**. A host at 1.3 installs the add-in cleanly and then fails at runtime when FR-02 tries to read or write the stamp.

      This is the THIRD instance of one failure class in this project. Task 011 found the manifest advertising WordApi 1.1 while `WordAdapter.getItemId()` required 1.3. Task 010's escalation was the same shape on the bootstrap path (`Office.context.host` assumed populated). Treat the pattern as the finding, not just this instance.

      Spike-1's recommended fix is better than a version bump: use the **common API** plus a `CustomXmlParts` declaration — no WordApi floor increase, a LOWER effective floor (perpetual Office 2016), and confirmed support on Word web, Mac and iPad. Evaluate that against a straight 1.3 → 1.4 bump and choose with reasoning.

      **(2) The markup-vs-parts premise — unconfirmed, and FR-02 rests entirely on it.** Spike-1 flagged that the distinction between custom XML *markup* and custom XML *parts* is an assumption nobody has verified. If the stamp does not survive the round trip the way FR-02 assumes, the whole mechanism fails. Spike-1 sized it as a ten-minute check.

      Weight: the operator decided 2026-09-09 that legacy documents do not matter (dev only), which removes the forward-only objection and makes the stamp a candidate PRIMARY identity mechanism rather than a fallback. So FR-02 now carries more load, and this premise carries more risk.
    </background>
    <relevant-files>
      <file role="canonical-reference">projects/spaarkeai-word-add-in-r1/notes/spikes/spike-1-document-url.md</file>
      <file role="canonical-reference">projects/spaarkeai-word-add-in-r1/notes/011-word-manifest-migration.md</file>
      <file role="modify">src/client/office-addins/word/manifest.json</file>
      <file role="modify">src/client/office-addins/word/word-manifest.xml</file>
    </relevant-files>
    <dependencies>
      <dependency task="002" status="complete">Spike-1 surfaced both items and recommended the common-API fix shape.</dependency>
    </dependencies>
  </context>

  <constraints>
    <constraint source="project">Both manifests MUST stay in step. Task 011 established that `word-manifest.xml` is RETAINED as the rollback path — with sideload gone, a bad manifest cannot be swapped locally and recovery costs a full build + deploy cycle. Do not delete it, and do not let the two drift.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">Do NOT raise the WordApi floor unless you show that the common-API alternative genuinely cannot work. Raising the floor excludes hosts; Spike-1's recommendation LOWERS it. Justify whichever you pick.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">Verify the API-set requirement at its source — the `[Api set: ...]` annotation in `@types/office-js` and current Microsoft Learn — and cite both. Do not infer a requirement level from a sibling API or from a version number that "looks right". Inferring is how the previous two instances of this bug were introduced.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">Version-bump both manifests per the project's convention if you change them (JSON is 3-part SemVer, XML is 4-part), and keep `word/taskpane/index.tsx`'s `APP_VERSION` in step — task 011 established that linkage.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">This task does NOT implement FR-02. It clears the way for task 014. Do not write stamping code.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">Do NOT regress the production typecheck count (0 as of 2026-09-09) and do not touch `shared/taskpane/hooks/**` — task 018 owns that concurrently.</constraint>
  </constraints>

  <escalation>
    <trigger>The markup-vs-parts premise turns out to be FALSE — the stamp cannot survive the round trip as FR-02 assumes. STOP. That invalidates FR-02's design and, with the operator's stamp-as-primary direction, re-opens the whole identity question. It is an operator decision, not something to engineer around.</trigger>
    <trigger>Confirming the premise genuinely requires a live Word host and cannot be settled from documentation plus the type definitions. Report exactly what must be observed and add it to the operator probe from Spike-1 §8 rather than guessing.</trigger>
    <trigger>Neither the common-API route nor a 1.4 bump covers all target hosts. That is a host-support scope decision — surface it rather than silently dropping a host.</trigger>
  </escalation>

  <acceptance-criteria>
    <criterion testable="true">Both Word manifests declare exactly what the stamp mechanism requires, with the chosen approach (common API + `CustomXmlParts`, or a WordApi 1.4 bump) justified in notes against the alternative.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">The requirement level is cited to BOTH the `@types/office-js` `[Api set: ...]` annotation and a dated Microsoft Learn page — no inferred versions.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">The markup-vs-parts premise is recorded as CONFIRMED or REFUTED with evidence, or as REQUIRES-LIVE-HOST with the exact observation added to the Spike-1 §8 operator probe.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">`word-manifest.xml` is retained and consistent with `manifest.json`; versions bumped per convention and `APP_VERSION` kept in step.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">`npm run build` succeeds and the emitted Word manifest has no unsubstituted placeholders.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">`npm run typecheck` shows production errors still at 0.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="false">Task 014's status is annotated with the outcome — green light, or the blocker that stops it.</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>

  <outputs>
    <output type="code">src/client/office-addins/word/manifest.json</output>
    <output type="doc">projects/spaarkeai-word-add-in-r1/notes/019-customxml-premise-and-manifest.md</output>
  </outputs>
  <notes>
    Completed 2026-09-09. Full record: projects/spaarkeai-word-add-in-r1/notes/019-customxml-premise-and-manifest.md

    (1) PREMISE: CONFIRMED, and settled WITHOUT a live host — the second escalation trigger did not need
    to fire. Two independent lines. (a) Microsoft's own i4i explainer (Gray Knowlton, GM Office product
    management, ms.date 2009-12-23, updated 2024-11-06) states that what was removed is the "Custom XML
    markup tags" and that "Content Controls and XML data stored within DOCX or DOCM files will not be
    affected by this change"; the Open XML SDK models the two as different classes in different
    namespaces (Wordprocessing.CustomXmlBlock vs Packaging.CustomXmlPart); and Microsoft INTRODUCED
    Word.Document.customXmlParts in WordApi 1.4 (Aug 2022), 12 years after the removal.
    (b) EMPIRICAL, in-repo: across all 48 .docx/.dotx/.docm in the worktree, w:customXml MARKUP occurs
    0 times, while /customXml/itemN.xml PARTS occur in 6 real documents — decisively including a
    THIRD-PARTY http://customooxmlschemas.google.com/ part that survived FOUR modern-Word save cycles
    intact (cp:revision 4, 1820 w:rsid attributes, w15:docId, mc:Ignorable "w14 w15 w16se w16cid w16
    w16cex w16sdtdh wp14" — Word provably the last writer). A foreign-namespace part surviving Word
    round-trips IS FR-02's scenario. Spike-1 section 8 step 6b is therefore RETIRED, not deferred.
    Residual (design obligation, not a blocker): the Document Inspector's "Custom XML Data / Remove All"
    module can strip the stamp, so a missing stamp must be treated as normal and re-stampable.

    (2) MANIFEST: fixed WITHOUT raising the WordApi floor. Chose Spike-1's option 1 — the COMMON API plus
    a CustomXmlParts (1.1) declaration — over a 1.3 to 1.4 bump. Verified at source that CustomXmlParts'
    floor (perpetual Office 2016) is at or BELOW the already-declared WordApi 1.3 floor (Win 1612 / LTSC
    Office 2019), so it excludes ZERO hosts; whereas WordApi 1.4 (Win 2208 / LTSC Office 2024 / Mac+iPad
    16.64) would drop Office 2019 AND Office 2021 LTSC — the volume-licensed tier enterprise legal
    departments run. The third escalation trigger did not fire: the chosen route covers every target
    host. Requirement levels cited to BOTH the @types/office-js@1.0.568 [Api set: ...] / **Requirement
    set**: annotations AND dated Microsoft Learn pages, per constraint. One residual doubt, recorded and
    handed to the operator probe: Microsoft publishes no allow-list of requirement-set names nameable in
    the unified JSON manifest, so run it through the Teams validator / admin-center upload before merge.

    Changed: word/manifest.json (+CustomXmlParts, 1.0.7 to 1.0.8), word/word-manifest.xml (+Set,
    1.0.7.0 to 1.0.8.0, reasoning comment; RETAINED per constraint and in step with the JSON),
    word/taskpane/index.tsx (APP_VERSION 1.0.7 to 1.0.8). No FR-02 code written. shared/taskpane/hooks/**
    untouched (task 018 owns it concurrently). Verified: npm run build exit 0; emitted manifests correct
    with no unsubstituted placeholders; XML well-formedness validated via System.Xml.XmlDocument;
    production typecheck 0 to 0 (total 289 to 284, the entire delta inside task 018's useAnnounce.test.ts);
    eslint exit 0.

    (3) TASK 014: GO, inheriting 4 conditions — see notes section 6.

    (4) PATTERN-LEVEL FINDING (notes section 7): this is the THIRD instance of one failure class (011:
    manifest 1.1 vs code 1.3; 010: Office.context.host assumed populated; 019: manifest 1.3 vs
    customXmlParts AND settings at 1.4). Root cause each time: a capability level INFERRED rather than
    read at source. Two structural mitigations recommended, neither implemented here (out of scope for a
    pre-flight task): a uniform runtime capability guard, and — the durable fix — a build-time script
    that extracts every [Api set: ...] annotation reachable from the add-in's Office.js call sites and
    diffs it against both manifests, converting the whole bug class into a build failure.
  </notes>
</task>

